Offsets: extending an edge keeps a curve on a face that lies on the edge
all along the edge's own range where reprojecting it fails, instead of
letting SameParameter replace it (mitcad#1201).

The case: a loft from a square to a circle of radius 20
(BRepOffsetAPI_ThruSections) whose circle's seam falls on a side, offset
inwards closed. The loft splits that side into two faces tangent along
the edge between them, and that edge's offset is shared by the two halves'
offsets, its curves on both lines u = 10 with v the edge's parameter.
BRepOffset_Inter2d::ExtentEdge extends it for the intersections: each
curve is clipped to its surface's bounds, which differ (the halves'
offset surfaces are extended by different lengths, v to 1.245 and 1.250),
the 3D curve is rebuilt on the narrower one, and the other is reprojected
onto its surface (GeomProjLib::Curve2d). That projection failed, and
SameParameter then replaced the exact line by an approximation off by
0.2 mm in parameter (v = 1.0065 t - 0.0033 where the surfaces agree). The
edge came out with that curve on one half, its tolerance raised to 0.14 by
UpdateTolerance: the offset valid, but that half 3.27 mm2 short and the
volume 12.7 mm3 short at 0.5 mm (8.1 at 3 mm).

Where the projection fails, a curve that lies on the rebuilt 3D curve
within its approximation's deviation plus 10 * Precision::Confusion() at
33 parameters over the edge's own range (the original edge's, within the
extension) is now kept as it is (the new PCurveFollowsCurve); only
otherwise SameParameter runs as before. Where the projection succeeds,
and for every other curve, nothing changes. The offsets' edges stay within
0.001 mm, and the volume is that of the same loft with the seam on a
corner within 1e-5.
